meta {
  name: Search products
  type: http
  seq: 4
  tags: [
    Stock
  ]
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/v2/stock/product/search
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "query": "",
    "in_stock": true,
    "sort": "relevance",
    "page": 1,
    "per_page": 20
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.timestamp: isDefined
  res.body.error: isDefined
  res.body.data: isDefined
  res.body.meta: isDefined
}

docs {
  Products of the site found in the search index by text, rubric, brands, aspect values (`param_ids`: OR inside one aspect, AND between aspects), price, stock and discount, page by page (`meta.pager`), in the format of `stock/product/show`: prices and stock are current, not from the index. Every page is the window of the index: a product the index has in stock but the live data does not comes with `available: false` instead of dropping out, and a product closed for the site after the last indexing comes with `closed: true`. Only a product unlinked from the site tree after the last indexing is left out, until the next indexing: then its page is one product shorter and `meta.pager.total`, the count of the index, is larger than the products you get. Stop at `meta.pager.pages`, not at a short page. The storefront hides a product with `available: false` itself; a product without `variations` cannot be sold now. With `sort` by price, products without a current price (`min_price: null`) go last on each page. A product added since the last indexing is not found yet: `stock/product/id/list` reads the database. Facets of the same selection (values with counts, brands, price range) — `stock/facet/list`. Pages go down to 10 000 products: a deeper page (`page` × `per_page` over 10 000) gives 422; to get all products use `stock/product/id/list` and `stock/product/batch-show`.
}
